      "researchNote": "FY2025 full-year (calendar year, ended Dec 2025), from Intel's FY2025 10-K: CCG $32,228M (60.7%), DCAI $16,919M (31.9%), of $53.1B total revenue \u2014 both directly sourced line items. Intel actually simplified its segment reporting in Q1 2025, folding the old Network & Edge (NEX) segment into CCG/DCAI, so there's no separate NEX line anymore; the remaining ~7.4% is a residual covering Mobileye, Intel Foundry's external revenue, and consolidation eliminations \u2014 not individually broken out in the 10-K. One concrete data point worth surfacing: Intel Foundry's external (non-Intel) customer revenue was just $307M in 2025, underscoring how early-stage that business still is despite the 18A narrative. 'Customers' (Microsoft, Amazon) are Intel Foundry's publicly announced 18A design customers, still pre-volume. R&D% (26.7%) is FY2025 R&D expense ($14.191B) / revenue \u2014 down from FY2024's ~31% as Intel's 2024-2025 restructuring cut R&D headcount and spend. Capex: this reverses what I reported last time \u2014 Intel cut gross capex hard through 2024-2025 (down to an ~$18B guide), but on its Q2 2026 earnings call (~July 23, 2026, days before this data was compiled) raised 2026 guidance to over $20B, citing surging AI-driven demand and growing confidence in the 14A node \u2014 a genuine, recent reversal, not an inconsistency in this data.",
